[Music]  it's a pleasure for me to be back in the  uk  even virtually  my first visit to your country was when  i was a teenager in  1950  a memorable visit on any of you who were  alive in 1950 will remember there was  food rationing still going on then and i  recall  in our hotel in cambridge the waiter in  the hotel  offering my sister and me eggs for  breakfast we did not want eggs for  breakfast  and the waiter was astonished and said  don't you understand you can have eggs  you have an allotment of eggs this  morning but we didn't want eggs that was  food rationing and my memories from 1950  also include included london all of the  bombed out sites in london that as of  1950 still had not been reconstructed so  that was 1950.  i lived in the uk from 1958 to 1962  and  that was  shortly after suez  it was after the nottingham and notting  hill  race riots that were a shock your first  race riots um in retrospect what was  going on  in britain then while i was there 58 to  62 was that you were undergoing a  national identity crisis although  neither i nor my british friends really  understood it at the time  ever since then i've returned frequently  to your country my wife and i spent our  honeymoon  in britain  britain is the only country to which i  seriously considered  moving  and i have friendships with  british people going back 65 years so  those are long wonderful connections  with your country  as i said while i was living there for  those four years in the late 50s  in retrospect  you were undergoing an identity crisis  which  is the subject of my most recent book  upheaval and something that's been much  on my mind my book upheaval um took a  comparative view of  crises now  what do i mean by a comparative view  most history books  are single case studies  they look at  one country in a narrow slice of time so  you get lots of books on late 19th  century germany or mid 16 16th century  england  instead i don't write single case  studies i write comparative histories my  book gun germs and steel  compared the histories of people on  different continents  my book collapsed compared different  societies that did or did not collapse  my book the world until yesterday  i've looked at traditional societies  tribal societies compared with modern  societies  well single case study history books and  comparative histories  each has its advantages and each has its  disadvantages  a single case study can devote all of  its  543 pages to late 19th century germany  and of course can go into far more  detail than can a comparative book in  which late 19th century germany just  gets 25 pages  in a book that looks at 11 other  countries as well  so the advantage of a single case  studies you can get far more detail  the advantage of a comparative study is  that questions arise and answers suggest  themselves that would never appear in a  single case study so  think of all those books about the  american civil war we have long books  about our civil war books of 500 700 900  pages those books  because they consider nothing except the  american civil war  can devote  three pages to the second day of the  battle of gettysburg go into enormous  detail but what those single case study  books do single case accounts of the  american civil war what they cannot do  is profit from the perspective of  looking at other civil wars and so those  books about the american civil war never  asked the question at the end of the  american civil war the victors did not  kill the losers there was only one  person on the losing side who was killed  that was the commandant of the terrible  prisoner of war camp  but at the ends of other civil wars the  spanish civil war the finnish civil war  so many other civil wars the victors did  kill the losers why did that not happen  at the end of the american civil war  that's an example of the questions that  arise in comparative studies that don't  arise in single case studies and so  there's a quip among historians  if you study just one society you'll end  up understanding no society  my books are comparative studies of  history with their advantages and with  their disadvantages  my most recent book  about crises national crises identity  crisis you may wonder did jared start to  write that book motivated by the brexit  vote in the uk or did i start to write  the book just after the november 2020  u.s elections or after covid emerged 14  months ago  no my book was not provoked by those  recent crises instead i began to work on  the subject already in the year 2013  after the publication of my previous  book the world until yesterday and i was  motivated to to  write to think about national political  crises because of my experience of  crises in the countries in which i've  lived and that i know best  in retrospect when i think of the  countries where i've lived  in each of those countries there was a  crisis either going on or  i was in the run-up to the crisis or i  was living in the country in the  aftermath of the crisis for example  i was living in chile in the run-up to  the 1973 chilean coup d'etat  i began working in indonesia in the  aftermath of indonesia's 1965 genocide  when i first visited finland in the  summer of 1959 my finnish hosts were the  veterans and widows and orphans of  finland's war went to war against the  soviet union  i began working in australia in 1964 as  australia was slowly  starting to dismantle its white  australia policy and i was living in  germany on the day that the berlin wall  was erected  um any of you who think that that  correlation suggests causation may be  wondering does jared diamond living in a  country um  is that a cause of a crisis my presence  and no correlation does not prove  causation why living in countries does  not provoke crises it's instead that  countries not in frequently experienced  crises often at least once a generation  and so just by chance my living in  countries has been associated either  with the aftermath or the occurrence or  the winding dam of a crisis  those crises are gut-wrenching  histories in their own right but they  also raise practical questions of why  some countries deal better with crisis  than to other countries to continue  watching this video click the link in  the top left or in the description below  or visit iai dot tv for more debates and  talks from the world's leading thinkers  on today's biggest ideas  you

